      "assistant": {
        "Response": {
          "message_id": "e6267ca3-443f-474c-9883-e9e056f1acf3",
          "content": "Good topic. The networking difference is the core reason people run into issues with Docker Desktop on Linux.\n\n**Regular Docker Engine (native)**\n- Docker daemon runs directly on the Linux host\n- `docker0` bridge network is created on the host\n- Containers are reachable from the host via their bridge IP\n- `host` network mode works — container shares the host's network namespace directly\n- `localhost` inside a container can reach the host via the gateway IP (usually `172.17.0.1`)\n\n**Docker Desktop on Linux**\n- Runs inside a lightweight VM (using QEMU/KVM)\n- The Docker daemon runs *inside that VM*, not on your host\n- All containers live inside the VM's network namespace\n- `host` network mode is effectively useless — it binds to the VM's network, not your Linux host\n- `host.docker.internal` DNS name is provided to reach the host machine from inside a container (same as Mac/Windows behavior)\n- Port mappings (`-p 8080:8080`) still work fine — Desktop proxies them through to your host\n\n**Practical implications:**\n- If your services use `host` network mode in `docker-compose.yml`, they won't behave as expected\n- Services inside containers can't reach host-local processes via `172.17.0.1` — use `host.docker.internal` instead\n- Container-to-container communication within the same Compose network works normally\n\nWhat specific issue are you running into? That'll help narrow down what's actually broken."
        }
      },
