          "content": "Right, this is a known pain point. The issue is that minikube runs inside Docker Desktop's VM, so `minikube tunnel` (which normally creates a route on the host) can't bridge back through the VM layer to your actual Linux host.\n\n**Your options:**\n\n**1. Switch minikube driver to `kvm2` or `virtualbox` (recommended)**\n\nThis runs minikube in its own VM directly on your host, bypassing Docker Desktop entirely. `minikube tunnel` works properly here.\n\n```bash\nminikube stop\nminikube delete\nminikube start --driver=kvm2\n```\n\nYou'd need `kvm2` driver installed:\n```bash\nsudo apt install qemu-kvm libvirt-daemon-system\ncurl -LO https://storage.googleapis.com/minikube/releases/latest/docker-machine-driver-kvm2\nsudo install docker-machine-driver-kvm2 /usr/local/bin/\n```\n\n**2. Use `minikube tunnel` + `/etc/hosts` (workaround, stays on Docker driver)**\n\nSometimes works with Docker Desktop on Linux if you run:\n```bash\nminikube tunnel\n```\nThen add the external IP to `/etc/hosts`. But this is unreliable with Docker Desktop due to the VM layer.\n\n**3. Use `NodePort` instead of `Ingress` (quick workaround)**\n\nExpose your `api-gateway` as `NodePort` and access via `minikube ip`:\n```bash\nminikube service api-gateway -n portfolio --url\n```\n\nThis works regardless of the driver since minikube handles the proxying.\n\n**4. Use `minikube kubectl -- port-forward`**\n\nSimple but manual — forward specific services to localhost ports directly.\n\n---\n\nThe cleanest long-term fix is switching to the `kvm2` driver. It gives you proper networking without abandoning Docker Desktop for your regular Docker workflows (they can coexist). What's your preference?"
