    "> /context show",
    "> I have Ubuntu 24.04.4 LTS and node v18.20.8. How do I upgrade node?",
    "The easiest way is via `nvm`:\n\n**If you don't have nvm:**\n```bash\ncurl -o- https://raw.githubusercontent.com/nvm-sh/nvm/v0.40.2/install.sh | bash\nsource ~/.bashrc\n```\n\n**Install and use the latest LTS:**\n```bash\nnvm install --lts\nnvm use --lts\nnvm alias default lts/*\n```\n\nOr a specific version:\n```bash\nnvm install 22\nnvm use 22\nnvm alias default 22\n```\n\nIf you already have nvm, just skip the install step. The `alias default` line makes it persist across new terminal sessions.\n[Tool uses: none]",
    "> I would like to opne discussion about an issue that I have in my local development machine. This is Ubuntu 24.04.4 LTS. For simplicity I opted to install Docker Desktop and not regular installation. Can you explain the differences between the two instalations in networking aspect?",
    "Good topic. The networking difference is the core reason people run into issues with Docker Desktop on Linux.\n\n**Regular Docker Engine (native)**\n- Docker daemon runs directly on the Linux host\n- `docker0` bridge network is created on the host\n- Containers are reachable from the host via their bridge IP\n- `host` network mode works — container shares the host's network namespace directly\n- `localhost` inside a container can reach the host via the gateway IP (usually `172.17.0.1`)\n\n**Docker Desktop on Linux**\n- Runs inside a lightweight VM (using QEMU/KVM)\n- The Docker daemon runs *inside that VM*, not on your host\n- All containers live inside the VM's network namespace\n- `host` network mode is effectively useless — it binds to the VM's network, not your Linux host\n- `host.docker.internal` DNS name is provided to reach the host machine from inside a container (same as Mac/Windows behavior)\n- Port mappings (`-p 8080:8080`) still work fine — Desktop proxies them through to your host\n\n**Practical implications:**\n- If your services use `host` network mode in `docker-compose.yml`, they won't behave as expected\n- Services inside containers can't reach host-local processes via `172.17.0.1` — use `host.docker.internal` instead\n- Container-to-container communication within the same Compose network works normally\n\nWhat specific issue are you running into? That'll help narrow down what's actually broken.\n[Tool uses: none]",
    "> I have minikube docker container running. Inside my kinikube I have different namespaces, together with portfolio-monorepo. I would like to expose an entry point for the application via Ingress. This is not possible with Docker Desktop installation.",
    "Right, this is a known pain point. The issue is that minikube runs inside Docker Desktop's VM, so `minikube tunnel` (which normally creates a route on the host) can't bridge back through the VM layer to your actual Linux host.\n\n**Your options:**\n\n**1. Switch minikube driver to `kvm2` or `virtualbox` (recommended)**\n\nThis runs minikube in its own VM directly on your host, bypassing Docker Desktop entirely. `minikube tunnel` works properly here.\n\n```bash\nminikube stop\nminikube delete\nminikube start --driver=kvm2\n```\n\nYou'd need `kvm2` driver installed:\n```bash\nsudo apt install qemu-kvm libvirt-daemon-system\ncurl -LO https://storage.googleapis.com/minikube/releases/latest/docker-machine-driver-kvm2\nsudo install docker-machine-driver-kvm2 /usr/local/bin/\n```\n\n**2. Use `minikube tunnel` + `/etc/hosts` (workaround, stays on Docker driver)**\n\nSometimes works with Docker Desktop on Linux if you run:\n```bash\nminikube tunnel\n```\nThen add the external IP to `/etc/hosts`. But this is unreliable with Docker Desktop due to the VM layer.\n\n**3. Use `NodePort` instead of `Ingress` (quick workaround)**\n\nExpose your `api-gateway` as `NodePort` and access via `minikube ip`:\n```bash\nminikube service api-gateway -n portfolio --url\n```\n\nThis works regardless of the driver since minikube handles the proxying.\n\n**4. Use `minikube kubectl -- port-forward`**\n\nSimple but manual — forward specific services to localhost ports directly.\n\n---\n\nThe cleanest long-term fix is switching to the `kvm2` driver. It gives you proper networking without abandoning Docker Desktop for your regular Docker workflows (they can coexist). What's your preference?\n[Tool uses: none]",
    "> I would like to make native docker installation instead of Docker Desktop. Can this be achievend?",
    "Yes, straightforward process. You remove Docker Desktop and install Docker Engine natively.\n\n**1. Remove Docker Desktop**\n```bash\nsudo apt remove docker-desktop\nrm -rf ~/.docker/desktop\nsudo rm /usr/local/bin/com.docker.cli\nsudo apt purge docker-desktop\n```\n\n**2. Install Docker Engine**\n```bash\n# Add Docker's official GPG key and repo\nsudo apt-get update\nsudo apt-get install ca-certificates curl\nsudo install -m 0755 -d /etc/apt/keyrings\nsudo curl -fsSL https://download.docker.com/linux/ubuntu/gpg -o /etc/apt/keyrings/docker.asc\nsudo chmod a+r /etc/apt/keyrings/docker.asc\n\necho \\\n  \"deb [arch=$(dpkg --print-architecture) signed-by=/etc/apt/keyrings/docker.asc] \\\n  https://download.docker.com/linux/ubuntu \\\n  $(. /etc/os-release && echo \"$VERSION_CODENAME\") stable\" | \\\n  sudo tee /etc/apt/sources.list.d/docker.list > /dev/null\n\nsudo apt-get update\nsudo apt-get install docker-ce docker-ce-cli containerd.io docker-buildx-plugin docker-compose-plugin\n```\n\n**3. Add your user to the docker group (avoid sudo)**\n```bash\nsudo usermod -aG docker $USER\nnewgrp docker\n```\n\n**4. Verify**\n```bash\ndocker run hello-world\n```\n\nAfter this, `minikube tunnel` and Ingress will work properly with the `docker` driver since the daemon runs natively on your host.\n[Tool uses: none]",
    "> Once I uninstall Docker Desktop, what data will I loose?",
    "You'll lose:\n\n- **Container instances** — any running or stopped containers\n- **Images** — all pulled/built images (you'll need to rebuild or re-pull)\n- **Volumes** — all named and anonymous volumes and their data\n- **Networks** — custom networks (default ones recreate automatically)\n- **Docker Compose state** — any running stacks\n\n**What you keep:**\n- Your `docker-compose.yml` files and `Dockerfile`s (they're just files on disk)\n- Any data you explicitly bind-mounted to host directories (e.g., `-v /host/path:/container/path`)\n- Your minikube cluster config in `~/.minikube` (though the cluster itself runs in Docker, so it'll be gone too)\n\n**Before uninstalling, consider:**\n- Export any important images: `docker save myimage | gzip > myimage.tar.gz`\n- Back up any volume data you care about\n- Run `minikube delete` cleanly before removing Docker Desktop\n\nFor your portfolio monorepo, since everything is built from source and images can be rebuilt with `./infra/build-portfolio.sh`, you likely don't have anything critical to save.\n[Tool uses: none]",
    "> I removed Docker Desktop and insalled native docker. How ever docker daemon is not running: docker ps> \nfailed to connect to the docker API at unix:///home/roman/.docker/desktop/docker.sock; check if the path is correct and if the daemon is running: dial unix /home/roman/.docker/desktop/docker.sock: connect: no such file or directory",
    "The error shows your Docker CLI is still pointing to the old Desktop socket path. Two things to fix:\n\n**1. Start the Docker daemon**\n```bash\nsudo systemctl enable docker\nsudo systemctl start docker\n```\n\n**2. Fix the socket path**\n\nThe native Docker socket is at `/var/run/docker.sock`, not the Desktop path. You likely have a leftover context from Docker Desktop:\n\n```bash\ndocker context use default\n```\n\nThen verify:\n```bash\ndocker ps\n```\n\nIf that doesn't work, check your current context:\n```bash\ndocker context ls\n```\n\nAnd explicitly unset any override:\n```bash\nunset DOCKER_HOST\n```\n[Tool uses: none]",
